WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.880
Machine knitting a sweater
should be easy, right?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.640
Just cast on, throw some yarn
at the machine, and let it do its thing.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.120
And voila, you have the perfect sweater.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.960
If only it worked that way.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.840
Too many knitters believe myths that lead
to frustrations and wasted time,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.127
like thinking the machine does all
the work or that any yarn will

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.720
magically knit up beautifully.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.560
And let's not even talk
about skipping swatching.

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:34.247
I see so many knitters running before they
can walk, diving into knitting a sweater

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.760
without building the right skills first.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.080
And that's when things
start to unravel, literally.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.480
But don't worry, if this sounds
familiar, you're not alone.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.240
At Knitted Now, we help machine knitters
like you build confidence,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.927
master techniques,
and avoid these mistakes with our

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.720
step-by-step success path,
skill builders, and all of our tutorials.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.120
And to make sure you start off
on the right foot,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.600
I've put together
a Sweater Planning Worksheet,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.920
a handy printable guide that walks you
through all the important decisions

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.760
before you start knitting.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.280
Grab your copy for free using
the link in the description.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.200
So let's dive into myths and mistakes.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.520
Let's start with the biggest myth.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.680
The machine does all the work.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.560
Well, wouldn't that be nice?

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.440
But in reality, your machine is only
as good as the knitter using it.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.680
You control the knitting stitches.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.920
You control the garment assembly.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.047
Think of it like cooking.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.520
Having a fancy-dancy kitchen doesn't
automatically make you a great chef.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.727
It knit It Now, we teach you how to take
control of your machine so you're the one

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.640
in charge, not just hoping for the best.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.560
Another common myth that any yarn
will work on any knitting machine.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.567
Have you ever your head stitches drop,
yarn gets stuck, or your machine

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.400
just refused to knit smoothly?

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.480
Chances are you are using the wrong yarn.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.567
Each machine has a specific gage range,
and some fibers just don't work

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.200
well with certain machines.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.200
That's why it's crucial to check your
machine specifications,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.440
and most importantly, swatch before
committing to a full sweater.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.440
Speaking of swatching, Swatching.

00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:35.800
Let's talk about one of the biggest
mistakes I see: skipping it altogether.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:37.800
Now, I get it.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.240
You're anxious to jump in and get netting.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.240
Skipping your swatch is like hanging
a picture without measuring first.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.207
You might get lucky,
but more often than not, it ends up

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.080
crooked, off-center, or just plain wrong.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:57.480
Taking a few extra minutes to swatch is
like using a level and a measuring tape.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.560
It ensures everything lined lines
up perfectly before you commit.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.840
Your swatch tells you everything: gage,
fabric drape, and potential yarn issues.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.927
Without it, you're just guessing.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.800
And trust me, guessing doesn't
lead to good fitting sweaters.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.127
At Knit It Now, it isn't just encouraged,
it's a crucial step in our

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.580
sweater knitting process.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.880
That's why we introduce the
Get Acquainted Swatch in our classroom.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.527
Available available exclusively
to members, this guided exercise walks you

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.567
through swatching the right way,
helping you understand your machine, yarn,

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:42.840
and tension before you even
cast on for a full project.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.520
No more Passwork, just reliable results.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.240
Are you serious about knitting
sweaters that you'd love to wear?

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.000
Make sure you check out the get-acquainted
swatch technique in our classroom.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.440
The link is in the description.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.640
Next up, a mistake of diving
into a pattern without reading it first.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.080
Patterns are like roadmaps.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.480
If you don't check where you're going,
you might end up lost.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.720
Before casting on, take a few minutes
to read through the whole pattern.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.167
Highlight key instructions and make sure
you understand all

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.200
the techniques involved.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.487
This simple habit will save you hours
of frustration and help

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.560
you knit with confidence.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.040
And finally, let's talk
about finishing techniques.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.160
A beautifully knit sweater can still look
homemade if the seams are bulky,

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.920
the neckline is uneven,
or the hems aren't finished right.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.160
Taking the time to seam neatly C,
block your pieces, and add details makes

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.000
all of the difference between,
I made this, and, Wait, you made this?

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.680
If finishing techniques intimidate you,
we've got a full library of tutorials

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.760
Inside Knitted Now to walk
you through every step.

